  "query_preview": "..\nToday through Thursday...\n\nToday will see upper level ridging begin to build over the Central\nRockies bringing higher heights into Montana. Monsoonal flow will\ncontinue to bring much above moisture into the region with PWATS\naround 200% of normal. These ingredients bring a 20-50% chance \nfor thunderstorms starting this afternoon. Decreased shear values\ndue to building heights will limit severe storm potential. Slow \nmoving storms with high moisture content means torrential rain \nwill be the biggest threat with these storms. The Weather \nPrediction Center has issued a level 1 of 4 risk for excessive \nrainfall for areas west of Billings.\n\nUpper level ridging peaks Saturday increasing temperatures into \nthe mid to high 90s F. Scattered showers and general \nthunderstorms are expected to bring the potential for torrential\nrain. The Weather Prediction Center has issued a level 1 of 4 risk\nfor excessive rainfall for locations southwest of Billings. Sunday\nand Monday see an upper level wave depress the upper level ridge.\nDownsloping 700 mb winds Sunday will keep temperatures above\naverage in the mid to high 90s F. This pattern will push the\nanomalously high moisture further to the south keeping it out of\nMontana. Precipitation chances are expected to be confined to the\nmountains and foothills these days. A cold front is expected to\nmove through Monday bringing surface high pressure. This will \ncool high temperatures into the 90s F Monday and 80s to low 90s F \nTuesday. \n\nMiddle to late next week, ensembles are in good agreement that\nupper level ridging will build back and return monsoonal moisture.\nThe exact nature in which the ridge builds back is uncertain,\nthough there is general agreement that much above average \ntemperatures will not return. There is higher confidence that\nmonsoonal moisture will bring the return of daily shower and\nthunderstorm chances. Torgerson",
